body:
Feature Request / Improvement
Problem. iceberg-aws declares the AWS SDK as compileOnly and the docs tell users to bring either the SDK bundle or "individual AWS client packages (Glue, S3, DynamoDB, KMS, STS)" (docs/docs/aws.md, "Enabling AWS Integration"). For S3FileIO alone, the set that is actually required at runtime is s3 plus kms and sts; glue and dynamodb are not needed. With only s3 (and an HTTP client) on the classpath, S3FileIO.initialize fails before any S3 call:
java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: software/amazon/awssdk/services/kms/model/EncryptionAlgorithmSpec
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsProperties.<clinit>(AwsProperties.java:231)
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsClientFactories$DefaultAwsClientFactory.<init>(AwsClientFactories.java:101)
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsClientFactories.<clinit>(AwsClientFactories.java:50)

Add kms and it becomes:
java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: software/amazon/awssdk/services/sts/model/Tag
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsProperties.toStsTags(AwsProperties.java:487)
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsProperties.<init>(AwsProperties.java:302)
    at org.apache.iceberg.aws.AwsClientFactories$DefaultAwsClientFactory.initialize(AwsClientFactories.java:179)

Add sts and initialization succeeds; glue and dynamodb make no difference. Verified on 1.12.0 with SDK 2.54.17 (JDK 21); AwsProperties, AwsClientFactories and the iceberg-aws build block are unchanged on main (2742348).
Cause. S3FileIO.initialize -> S3FileIOAwsClientFactories.initialize falls back to AwsClientFactories.from(...) when s3.client-factory-impl is unset -> AwsClientFactories.<clinit> builds DefaultAwsClientFactory, whose constructor and initialize eagerly build AwsProperties. AwsProperties has a static field of type kms.model.EncryptionAlgorithmSpec and its constructor calls toStsTags, so the KMS and STS model classes are loaded even though the S3-only path never uses them.
This is a smaller, precise instance of #7516 (avoid loading all AWS client dependencies): the S3-only factory from #7590 helps only when s3.client-factory-impl is set explicitly; the default path still pulls KMS and STS.
Reproduction. Classpath: org.apache.iceberg:iceberg-aws:1.12.0, iceberg-core:1.12.0, software.amazon.awssdk:s3:2.54.17, software.amazon.awssdk:url-connection-client:2.54.17; no kms, no sts.
import java.util.Map;
import org.apache.iceberg.aws.s3.S3FileIO;

public class InitS3FileIO {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    try (S3FileIO io = new S3FileIO()) {
      io.initialize(Map.of());
      System.out.println("initialize OK");
    } catch (Throwable t) {
      System.out.println("initialize FAILED: " + t);
      t.printStackTrace(System.out);
    }
  }
}
Proposal.

Build AwsProperties lazily in DefaultAwsClientFactory: keep the property map, construct AwsProperties on first use in glue(), kms() and dynamo() (3 call sites plus a private accessor; about 15 changed lines in one class). With that change the program above prints initialize OK on the S3-only classpath, and a real exists() call builds the S3 client and fails only with ConnectException against a closed port. Prototype diff below (compiled against the released jars and shadowed on the classpath; not yet run through the project's own test suite / Spotless).
Correct docs/docs/aws.md: for S3FileIO the minimal set is s3 + an HTTP client today (plus kms/sts until (1) lands); Glue and DynamoDB are only needed for GlueCatalog / DynamoDbCatalog / the DynamoDB lock manager.

Anything that constructs AwsProperties directly (AwsKeyManagementClient, assume-role factories, SigV4 REST auth) legitimately still needs KMS/STS; (1) only stops the S3-only default path from loading them. I can open the PR for both parts; a regression test would load S3FileIO through a class loader that refuses software.amazon.awssdk.services.kms.* and sts.*.

Prototype patch against the 1.12.0 sources
--- a/aws/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/aws/AwsClientFactories.java
+++ b/aws/src/main/java/org/apa […]

Apache Iceberg version
1.11.0
Query engine
Spark
Please describe the bug 🐞
VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy can throw a NullPointerException on the first requests that go through a new ADLSFileIO when several threads send them at the same moment.
The affected file is identical in 1.12.0 and on main.
java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "com.azure.core.http.policy.AzureSasCredentialPolicy.process(com.azure.core.http.HttpPipelineCallContext, com.azure.core.http.HttpPipelineNextPolicy)" because "this.azureSasCredentialPolicy" is null
	at org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2.VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy.lambda$process$0(VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy.java:50)
	at reactor.core.publisher.MonoDefer.subscribe(MonoDefer.java:45)
	at reactor.core.publisher.MonoIgnoreThen$ThenIgnoreMain.subscribeNext(MonoIgnoreThen.java:241)

Cause
In maybeUpdateCredential, the volatile azureSasCredential is published before the plain field azureSasCredentialPolicy is assigned:
private volatile AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
private AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
...
if (azureSasCredential == null) {
  synchronized (this) {
    if (azureSasCredential == null) {
      this.azureSasCredential = new AzureSasCredential(sasToken);
      this.azureSasCredentialPolicy = new AzureSasCredentialPolicy(azureSasCredential, false);
      return Mono.empty();
    }
  }
}
azureSasCredential.update(sasToken);
return Mono.empty();
A second thread can read azureSasCredential as non-null, skip the lock, and reach azureSasCredentialPolicy.process(...) (line 50, and processSync has the same pattern) while the first thread has not yet assigned the policy. The policy field is also read without any happens-before edge to its write. #13730 added the volatile and the double-checked lock on the credential field, but not on the field that process dereferences.
ADLSFileIO.buildClient creates one policy per storage account per ADLSFileIO instance, so any burst of parallel first requests on a new file IO is exposed, for example parallel manifest reads right after a table load.
Where I saw it
Production, Azure, REST catalog with vended credentials: a Spark based compaction job over roughly 3,300 tables per hourly run failed one or two tables per run, at random, with this exception. The step failed about 0.4 s after it started, and the next run compacted the same table. Log line before the NPE, from one occurrence:
ERROR org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2.ADLSInputStream - Failed to open input stream for file <redacted>/metadata/<uuid>-m0.avro, range bytes=0-

Reproducer
A JUnit 5 test in the org.apache.iceberg.azure.adlsv2 package drives the real class through a real HttpPipeline with a stub HttpClient. In each round it creates a fresh VendedAzureSasCredentialPolicy (the provider is a subclass returning Mono.just(token)) and releases N threads together on a spin barrier, each doing pipeline.send(request).block().
Against the unmodified 1.11.0 jar:

Threads
Rounds
Result

8
200,000
31,427 NPEs in 19,286 rounds

8 and 4
20,000
the test fails in 8 of 8 runs (11 to 102 failing rounds)

The NPE message and the line 50 frame are the same as in production. With a small round count the hit rate depends on JIT warm-up (one 2,000 round run at 8 threads hit nothing), so the test needs about 20,000 rounds (about 3 s).
Proposed fix
Make the field that process dereferences the volatile guard, and assign the credential first:
-  private volatile AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
-  private AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
+  private AzureSasCredential azureSasCredential;
+  private volatile AzureSasCredentialPolicy azureSasCredentialPolicy;
...
-              if (azureSasCredential == null) {
+              if (azureSasCredentialPolicy == null) {
                 synchronized (this) {
-                  if (azureSasCredential == null) {
+                  if (azureSasCredentialPolicy == null) {
With this change the same test ran 1,000,000 rounds at […]

Apache Iceberg version
main (development)
Query engine
None
Please describe the bug 🐞
Problem
truncate[W] on strings truncates to W code points (UnicodeUtil.truncateString), but TruncateString.project and projectStrict decide how to project startsWith/notStartsWith by comparing the prefix's String.length() (UTF-16 chars) with W.
A prefix with supplementary characters such as emoji can have length() == W and still have fewer than W code points. The projection then treats it as a full partition value and turns it into an equality, while the partition values of matching rows are longer.
Reproduce
Schema schema = new Schema(Types.NestedField.optional(1, "s", Types.StringType.get()));
PartitionSpec spec = PartitionSpec.builderFor(schema).truncate("s", 2).build();

// "😀" is 1 code point but 2 chars. A row "😀abc" lands in partition s_trunc = "😀a".
Projections.inclusive(spec).project(Expressions.startsWith("s", "😀"));
// actual:   ref(name="s_trunc") == "😀"         -> partition "😀a" is pruned
// expected: ref(name="s_trunc") startsWith "😀"

Projections.strict(spec).project(Expressions.notStartsWith("s", "😀"));
// actual:   ref(name="s_trunc") != "😀"         -> partition "😀a" is treated as all-matching
// expected: ref(name="s_trunc") notStartsWith "😀"
Impact

Inclusive projection prunes partitions that contain matching rows, so scans with such a startsWith filter silently return fewer rows.
Strict projection reports partitions as fully matching when none of their rows match, which affects residuals and partition-level decisions such as metadata-only deletes.

It only happens for string truncate partitions when the prefix's UTF-16 length equals the width but its code point count is smaller. ASCII and BMP-only prefixes are unaffected.
Proposed fix
Compare the prefix's code point count with the width in both methods. I have a fix with tests and can open a PR.
Willingness to contribute

 I can contribute a fix for this bug independently
 I would be willing to contribute a fix for this bug with guidance from the Iceberg community
 I cannot contribute a fix for this bug at this time

Apache Iceberg version
1.12.0 (latest release)
Query engine
Flink
Please describe the bug 🐞
What happens. We use a REST catalog that vends storage credentials (BigLake on GCS), and the job's own service
account has no direct bucket access. In that setup, a Flink batch query on a metadata table fails, for example
SELECT * FROM t$files. The JobManager plans the scan fine. Every TaskManager then fails reading the manifests with
403 storage.objects.get, and the error names the job's own service account. A normal data scan of the same table
works.
Why. The FLIP-27 source sends each split to the TaskManagers as JSON. For metadata-table tasks, that JSON
includes the FileIO, written by FileIOParser. FileIOParser writes only io.properties()
(L47)
and rebuilds the FileIO without credentials
(L79).
GCSFileIO keeps vended credentials in a separate field, not in properties()
(L85).
So on the TaskManager the rebuilt FileIO has no credentials and falls back to the environment's identity.
Data scans aren't affected, because their reader's FileIO travels by Java serialization, which keeps the credentials.
$entries and $all_manifests use the same parser, so they are probably affected too. S3FileIO stores
credentials the same way
(L114),
but we've only tested GCS. Related: #10735 added this JSON path, and #10926 fixed a similar gap in the same parser.
Repro (unit test, no cloud needed). The test builds a $files task whose GCSFileIO holds one credential, then
round-trips it through ScanTaskParser. The credentials come back empty on 1.11.0 and 1.12.0. The same task
round-tripped through Java serialization keeps them.
