Problem
Since 1.10.0, s3.preload-client-enabled (S3FileIOProperties.PRELOAD_CLIENT_ENABLED) no longer creates the S3 client in S3FileIO.initialize(), although its Javadoc still says: "This flag controls whether the S3 client will be initialized during the S3FileIO initialization, instead of default lazy initialization upon use."
Up to 1.9.x, initialize() called client() when the flag was set. #12799 moved client creation into PrefixedS3Client and the flag check into its constructor, but S3FileIO creates its PrefixedS3Clients lazily in clientByPrefix() on the first file operation. So initialize() no longer creates any client, and the flag only makes the sync client get built together with the client map. Nothing tests the eager behavior: TestS3FileIOProperties only checks that the flag is parsed.
Reproduction
This test fails on main:
private static final AtomicInteger S3_CLIENTS = new AtomicInteger();

@Test
void preloadCreatesClientInInitialize() {
  try (S3FileIO fileIO = new S3FileIO()) {
    fileIO.initialize(
        ImmutableMap.of(
            S3FileIOProperties.CLIENT_FACTORY,
            CountingFactory.class.getName(),
            S3FileIOProperties.PRELOAD_CLIENT_ENABLED,
            "true"));
    assertThat(S3_CLIENTS).hasValue(1);
  }
}

public static class CountingFactory implements S3FileIOAwsClientFactory {
  @Override
  public S3Client s3() {
    S3_CLIENTS.incrementAndGet();
    return Mockito.mock(S3Client.class);
  }

  @Override
  public S3AsyncClient s3Async() {
    return Mockito.mock(S3AsyncClient.class);
  }

  @Override
  public void initialize(Map<String, String> properties) {}
}
java.lang.AssertionError:
Expecting AtomicInteger(0) to have value:
  1
but did not.

Lake Formation
The only in-tree user is GlueCatalog.newTableOps, which sets the flag for every table when glue.lakeformation-enabled is true (#5756). The eager behavior is also what broke #10226 (create table) and #14766 (loading a missing table from Kafka Connect): newTableOps initializes the table's FileIO right away (today through FileIOTracker.track), so initialize() reached LakeFormationAwsClientFactory.isTableRegisteredWithLakeFormation() before the Glue table existed and threw Glue's EntityNotFoundException. Since #12799, S3FileIO.initialize() no longer calls the factory's s3(), so that path is gone.
Question
Does Lake Formation still need the client to be created in initialize()?

If it does: restore the client() call in S3FileIO.initialize() (storage credentials are already set by then, because CatalogUtil.loadFileIO calls setCredentials first), cover it with the test above, and keep GlueCatalog from requesting preload for a table that does not exist yet, so #10226 does not come back.
If it does not: deprecate the flag and stop setting it in GlueCatalog.

cc @xiaoxuandev @jackye1995 @singhpk234

Apache Iceberg version
main (1c99ae9); the same code is present since 1.7.0
Query engine
Kafka Connect
Please describe the bug 🐞
Channel.send() (and Channel.commitConsumerOffsets()) aborts the transaction and rethrows any exception from producer.send() / sendOffsetsToTransaction() / commitTransaction():

      iceberg/kafka-connect/kafka-connect/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/connect/channel/Channel.java

        Lines 98 to 117
      in
      1c99ae9

           synchronized (producer) {

             producer.beginTransaction();

             try {

               // NOTE: we shouldn't call get() on the future in a transactional context,

               // see docs for org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer

               recordList.forEach(producer::send);

               if (!sourceOffsets.isEmpty()) {

                 producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(

                     offsetsToCommit, KafkaUtils.consumerGroupMetadata(context));

               }

               producer.commitTransaction();

             } catch (Exception e) {

               try {

                 producer.abortTransaction();

               } catch (Exception ex) {

                 LOG.warn("Error aborting producer transaction", ex);

               }

               throw e;

             }

           }

TransactionAbortableException means only the current transaction failed: the producer is still valid and the client is expected to abort and retry in a new transaction (KIP-890, KIP-1050). Brokers can return it during normal operation, so transactional clients should expect it at any time.
Because the connector rethrows it, the task fails permanently:

Worker path: IcebergSinkTask.put() → CommitterImpl.save() → Worker.process() → Worker.receive() → Channel.send(). The exception escapes put(). It is not an org.apache.kafka.connect.errors.RetriableException, so WorkerSinkTask.deliverMessages() kills the task ("Task is being killed and will not recover until manually restarted") and it stays FAILED.
Coordinator path: Coordinator.process() / commit() → Channel.send() / commitConsumerOffsets(). CoordinatorThread terminates with the error, isFenced() is false, and the next save() throws NotRunningException, failing the task the same way.

Data is not lost (on restart the task seeks back to the last offsets committed in the transaction), but every affected task needs a manual restart. If many tasks hit the error at the same moment, all of them fail together.
Rethrowing as a Connect RetriableException is not enough on its own. By the time send() fails, Worker.receive() has already called sinkWriter.completeWrite() and used up the START_COMMIT event, so a redelivered put() would not resend the DataWritten / DataComplete events.
Proposed fix
Retry inside Channel.send() (and commitConsumerOffsets()). Everything needed is already in hand there: recordList (built from the events argument) and offsetsToCommit (built from sourceOffsets).
for (int attempt = 1; ; attempt++) {
  synchronized (producer) {
    producer.beginTransaction();
    try {
      recordList.forEach(producer::send);
      if (!offsetsToCommit.isEmpty()) {
        producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsetsToCommit, KafkaUtils.consumerGroupMetadata(context));
      }
      producer.commitTransaction();
      return;
    } catch (TransactionAbortableException e) {
      abortQuietly();
      if (attempt >= maxAttempts) {
        throw e;
      }
      // backoff, then retry
    } catch (Exception e) {
      abortQuietly();
      throw e; // fenced / fatal errors: unchanged behaviour
    }
  }
}
Why resending the same events is safe:

Records from the aborted transaction are not visible to the read_committed control-topic consumers, so the retry does not produce duplicates.
The DataWritten events only reference data files already written to storage. The retry does not touch those files again.
The source offsets are committed in the same transaction as the events, so a retry cannot […]

