Please describe the bug 🐞
After DROP PARTITION FIELD p and ADD PARTITION FIELD q, a file with p = NULL (old spec) and a file with q = NULL (new spec) are reported by the partitions table as one row with the new spec_id and summed counts; spec 0 disappears. With non-NULL values there is one row per spec. Reproduced on main (2e12b22, 2026-11-25) and 1.12.0, Spark 3.5.5.
Reproduction
CREATE TABLE local.t (id INT, p STRING, q STRING) USING iceberg PARTITIONED BY (p);
INSERT INTO local.t VALUES (1, NULL, 'old');
ALTER TABLE local.t DROP PARTITION FIELD p;
ALTER TABLE local.t ADD PARTITION FIELD q;
INSERT INTO local.t VALUES (2, 'new', NULL);
SELECT 'parts' AS marker, spec_id, partition, record_count, file_count FROM local.t.partitions ORDER BY spec_id;
SELECT 'files' AS marker, spec_id, partition FROM local.t.files ORDER BY spec_id;

-- control: non-NULL values
CREATE TABLE local.c (id INT, p STRING, q STRING) USING iceberg PARTITIONED BY (p);
INSERT INTO local.c VALUES (1, 'old', 'old');
ALTER TABLE local.c DROP PARTITION FIELD p;
ALTER TABLE local.c ADD PARTITION FIELD q;
INSERT INTO local.c VALUES (2, 'new', 'new');
SELECT 'control' AS marker, spec_id, partition, record_count, file_count FROM local.c.partitions ORDER BY spec_id;
Expected vs. actual

Expected
Actual

local.t.partitions
0  {"p":null,"q":null}  1  12  {"p":null,"q":null}  1  1
2  {"p":null,"q":null}  2  2

local.t.files
0 ..., 2 ...
0  {"p":null,"q":null}2  {"p":null,"q":null}

local.c.partitions (control)
one row per spec
0  {"p":"old","q":null}  1  12  {"p":null,"q":"new"}  1  1

The old-spec partition (p is NULL) and the new-spec partition (q is NULL) are different partitions, but they are collapsed into one row and the old spec_id 0 disappears.
